{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P6b8zJyNvJnJnPU4LESs2K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — policy_ambiguity_safety\n2\t\n3\t> 最后更新: 2026-05-20 19:30 UTC | 状态: 5-model analysis complete, C3/C4 framing revised\n4\t> 目标: EMNLP 2026 (ARR Aug cycle, DDL ~Aug 3)\n5\t> Dream: dream_20260520T112859 — best 3.5 / median 3.0 / worst 2.5 (pre-pivot; re-dream needed)\n6\t> 5-model: 2,997 episodes, C1 confirmed (+34.0pp, 5 models), C4 upgraded (model×type interaction)\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\t\n10\tPolicy ambiguity universally causes massive safety violations in LLM agents (+34.0pp across 5 architecturally diverse models), with a significant type hierarchy (incompleteness 53.6% >> coreferential 31.6%). Specification-layer types are more dangerous on average (Δ=6.08pp, p=0.017), but the deeper finding is a **model × type interaction**: authorization scope vulnerability varies 52pp across models (12% Claude to 64% GPT-5.4), revealing that safety training differentially mitigates specific ambiguity types, while incompleteness remains universally dangerous. This interaction connects Berry & Kamsties' nocuous ambiguity theory to modern safety alignment and suggests both type-aware triage AND model-aware risk assessment.\n11\t\n12\t## Title (Working)\n13\t\n14\t\"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n15\t\n16\tAlternative: \"Differential Nocuity: How Ambiguity Type and Model Architecture Shape Safety Violations in LLM Agents\"\n17\t\n18\tNote: Pivoted from \"Uniformly Nocuous\" → \"Specification-Layer Dominant\" (2-model) → \"Differential Nocuity with Model Interaction\" (5-model). The model × type interaction is the novel finding that elevates the paper beyond simple effect demonstration.\n19\t\n20\t## Core Claims\n21\t\n22\t### C1: Binary Effect (PRIMARY — confirmed, 5 models)\n23\tAmbiguous policy specifications cause massive safety violation rate increases compared to unambiguous matched variants. Effect: +34.0pp (5-model), McNemar OR=15.1 (CI: [10.78, 21.17]), p<0.0001. Holds across all 6 types and all 5 models.\n24\t- **Evidence**: 2,997 episodes across 5 models × 6 types × 2 conditions. Bootstrap CI: [+31.2pp, +36.9pp].\n25\t- **Per-model**: Claude +25.7pp, Qwen3 +32.4pp, GPT-4.1 +34.7pp, GPT-5.4 +38.3pp, DeepSeek +39.0pp — all p<10^-17.\n26\t- **Strength**: Extremely strong. Replicated across 5 architecturally diverse models (proprietary dense, open-source MoE, open-source dense), 6 types, 2 domains. No exceptions.\n27\t- **Causal backing**: Matched-pair design + perturbation control (plan_010, Δ_perturb=3.33pp, PASS) → strongest causal inference in the literature.\n28\t- **Logistic regression**: condition_amb coef=2.278, z=19.65, p<0.001 — ambiguity is the dominant predictor even after controlling for token delta, model, and type.\n29\t\n30\t### C2: Type Hierarchy (SECONDARY — confirmed, 5 models)\n31\t**PIVOTED from uniformity null.** 6-type hierarchy significant: omnibus χ²=28.02, p=3.6×10^-5, V=0.137 (small but well-powered: actual power=0.993, MDE=0.093). 3-tier structure emerges from TOST analysis.\n32\t- **Evidence**: 2,997 episodes (5 models × 6 types × 2 conditions, ambiguous only: 1,499 episodes)\n33\t  - **High tier**: incompleteness 53.6% (standout — universally dangerous across all models)\n34\t  - **Mid tier**: lexical 42.8%, auth_scope 41.6%, scopal 39.4%, CP 36.8% — pairwise equivalent at ±15pp\n35\t  - **Low tier**: coreferential 31.6% (lowest — partial disambiguation via tool responses)\n36\t- **TOST structure**: ±15pp: 8/15 equivalent, 7/15 non-equivalent. ±10pp: 4/15 equivalent, 11/15 non-equivalent. Types are NOT interchangeable.\n37\t- **V=0.137 context**: Below 0.20 threshold but well-powered (power=0.993). The type effect is real but small — the 3-tier structure captures it better than a single V statistic. The heterogeneity is driven by the extremes (incompleteness 53.6% vs coreferential 31.6% = 22pp gap).\n38\t- **Coreferential as boundary case**: 31.6% violation rate is the lowest but still substantial (+18.7pp vs unambiguous). Agents partially disambiguate via tool responses and cautious strategies. Conservative_refusal rate 6-11% (vs avg 2-3%).\n39\t- **Model-dependence of type rankings**: Type rankings are NOT identical across models. Incompleteness is top-2 for ALL models, but auth_scope ranges from #1 (GPT-5.4, DeepSeek) to #6 (Claude). See C4.\n40\t\n41\t### C3: Layer Effect (TERTIARY — suggestive, fragile under within-judge analysis)\n42\t**[2026-05-20 REVISED with 5-model data + within-judge check]** Specification-layer ambiguities cause more violations than linguistic-layer on average, but the effect is modest, model-dependent, and fragile under subgroup analysis.\n43\t- **Evidence (5-model aggregate)**: Spec 44.0% vs Ling 37.9%, Δ=6.08pp, CI [1.14, 11.02], χ²=5.73, p=0.017, V=0.062 (small), OR=1.29 [1.05, 1.58].\n44\t- **Within-judge fragility**: gpt-4.1 judged group (gpt-5.4+DeepSeek): Δ=+8.0pp, p=0.059 (marginal). gpt-5.4 judged group (gpt-4.1+Claude+Qwen3): Δ=+4.8pp, p=0.157 (n.s.). Pooled significance may partly reflect between-judge variance or insufficient within-group power. Paper表述: \"suggestive\" or \"a moderate tendency,\" NOT \"confirmed.\" Disclose within-judge non-significance in Limitations.\n45\t- **Per-model layer gaps** (THE KEY FINDING — feeds C4):\n46\t  - GPT-5.4: +14.0pp (spec 48.0% vs ling 34.0%) — strong\n47\t  - GPT-4.1: +12.0pp (spec 46.7% vs ling 34.7%) — strong\n48\t  - Claude: +3.3pp (spec 32.0% vs ling 28.7%) — weak\n49\t  - DeepSeek: +2.0pp (spec 50.7% vs ling 48.7%) — negligible\n50\t  - Qwen3: -0.9pp (spec 42.7% vs ling 43.6%) — reversed!\n51\t- **Root cause**: auth_scope is the primary driver. GPT models have extremely high auth_scope rates (46-64%), inflating their spec-layer average. Claude (12%) and Qwen3 (26%) have low auth_scope, so their spec-layer advantage disappears.\n52\t- **Framing decision**: C3 is NOT \"spec-layer nearly twice as dangerous\" (that was 2-model artifact). The correct framing is: \"Specification-layer types are significantly more dangerous on average (Δ=6.08pp, p=0.017), but this aggregate masks a model × type interaction where the gap varies from +14pp (GPT-5.4) to -1pp (Qwen3). The interaction, not the main effect, is the theoretically interesting finding (see C4).\"\n53\t- **Info delta as mediator** [MF-2]: Token delta significant in 5-model logistic regression (coef=0.022, z=3.49, p=0.0005). Spec-layer clauses require more tokens to disambiguate (auth_scope 36.1, incomp 34.6 vs coref 7.8 tokens). Info delta partially mediates but doesn't eliminate the condition effect (condition_amb coef=2.278 dominates).\n54\t- **Layer overlap persists**: CP (36.8%, spec) < scopal (39.4%, ling). Mid-tier cluster spans both layers. Layer effect is driven by extremes: incompleteness (53.6%, spec) vs coreferential (31.6%, ling).\n55\t- **ICC convergent evidence** [plan_006]: Per-type ICC mirrors layer hierarchy — Ling-layer ICC all excellent (scopal 0.989, lexical 0.981, coref 0.976), Spec-layer ICC all good (auth 0.857, CP 0.856, incomp 0.807). Spec-layer less reproducible — agents guess differently each run when external disambiguation channels are absent.\n56\t- **CL connection**: Parallels pragmatics vs semantics distinction. Good-enough processing (Ferreira et al. 2002). Disambiguation channel framework: linguistic ambiguity has partial external resolution; specification ambiguity forces guessing.\n57\t\n58\t### C4: Model × Type Interaction (UPGRADED — star finding, 5 models complete)\n59\t**[2026-05-20 DEFINITIVE, 5/5 models]** Models show significantly different vulnerability profiles despite universal binary effect. The interaction is the paper's most novel finding.\n60\t- **Model main effect**: Overall χ²=31.02, p=3.0×10^-6, V=0.102. Ambiguous-only: χ²=24.01, p=7.9×10^-5.\n61\t  - Vulnerability ranking: Claude 30.3% < GPT-5.4 41.0% ≈ GPT-4.1 40.7% < Qwen3 43.1% < DeepSeek 49.7%\n62\t  - Bootstrap CI confirms: Claude significantly lower than all others; DeepSeek significantly higher than GPT models.\n63\t- **Model × Type interaction matrix** (ambiguous condition, violation rates):\n64\t  ```\n65\t  Type                  GPT-5.4  GPT-4.1  Claude  Qwen3   DeepSeek\n66\t  authorization_scope     64%     46%      12%     26%     60%\n67\t  conditional_precedence  34%     36%      36%     40%     38%\n68\t  incompleteness          46%     58%      48%     62%     54%\n69\t  lexical                 42%     34%      32%     52%     54%\n70\t  coreferential           18%     32%      22%     42%     44%\n71\t  scopal                  42%     38%      32%     37%     48%\n72\t  ```\n73\t- **Key interaction patterns**:\n74\t  - **auth_scope**: 52pp range (Claude 12% → GPT-5.4 64%). The MODEL-DIFFERENTIATING type. Claude's RLHF appears to specifically train authorization boundary awareness.\n75\t  - **coreferential**: 26pp range (GPT-5.4 18% → DeepSeek 44%). Second most model-dependent. Open-source models struggle more with referential ambiguity.\n76\t  - **incompleteness**: Universally high (46-62%, 16pp range). ALL models fail on missing information — safety training doesn't help when information literally isn't there.\n77\t  - **CP**: Most model-invariant (34-40%, 6pp range). Explicit rule conflicts treated similarly by all architectures.\n78\t- **Per-model type χ²** (which models show type-specific vulnerability patterns):\n79\t  - GPT-5.4: χ²=23.44, p=0.0003, V=0.28 — strongest type differentiation (very high auth_scope, very low coref)\n80\t  - Claude: χ²=17.87, p=0.003, V=0.24 — strong differentiation (very low auth_scope, moderate others)\n81\t  - Qwen3: χ²=15.89, p=0.007, V=0.23 — moderate differentiation (high incomp+lex, low auth_scope)\n82\t  - GPT-4.1: χ²=9.89, p=0.078, V=0.18 — borderline\n83\t  - DeepSeek: χ²=6.31, p=0.277, V=0.14 — NOT significant (uniformly high across all types)\n84\t- **Fleiss κ = 0.384 (fair agreement)**: Models only moderately agree on which specific clauses trigger violations. The binary effect is universal, but the WHICH-clauses question is model-dependent.\n85\t- **Theoretical implications**: (1) Safety training differentially addresses specific ambiguity types — Claude's auth_scope robustness suggests authorization boundary handling was explicitly or implicitly trained. (2) Incompleteness is a \"safety training blind spot\" — all models fail equally. (3) DeepSeek's uniform vulnerability suggests less type-specific safety training.\n86\t- **Framing**: C4 from \"models agree\" → \"Models show systematically different vulnerability profiles. The binary effect is universal, but HOW models fail depends on the intersection of ambiguity type and safety training approach. auth_scope is the canary type — most sensitive to safety training quality.\"\n87\t- **Evidence from plan_005**: 2-model failure modes consistent (5/6 dominant modes identical). 5-model failure mode analysis pending.\n88\t- **Layer effect moderation**: C3's aggregate layer gap (6.08pp) is driven by GPT models' high auth_scope rates. The layer effect is itself model-dependent (GPT +12-14pp, Claude +3pp, Qwen3 reversed), making the MODEL × TYPE interaction more fundamental than the LAYER main effect.\n89\t\n90\t### C5: Error Analysis (CONFIRMED — EMNLP core)\n91\tDifferent ambiguity types produce qualitatively different failure modes with type-specific signatures. plan_005 (123+122 violation traces across gpt-5.4 and gpt-4.1, confidence=0.94).\n92\t- **Evidence**: plan_005 complete (gpt-5.4: 123 traces, gpt-4.1: 122 traces). Key findings:\n93\t  - **Type-specific failure signatures**: auth_scope → unauthorized_escalation (72%/83%); incompleteness → assumption_based_action (61%/66%); scopal → scope_misapplication (76%/74%); lexical → assumption_based_action (71%/53%); CP → scope_misapplication (41%/28%) + arbitrary_rule_selection (29%/28%); coreferential → scope_misapplication (44%) / assumption_based_action (38%)\n94\t  - **Cross-model consistency**: 5/6 types share the same dominant failure mode across gpt-5.4 and gpt-4.1. Only coreferential diverges (scope_misapplication vs assumption_based_action). Overall mode distribution nearly identical: both ~33% assumption_based_action, ~26-32% unauthorized_escalation, ~25-28% scope_misapplication. Failure mode profiles are type-specific, not model-specific.\n95\t  - **Layer mechanism**: Ling-layer dominated by scope_misapplication (39% ling avg vs 15% spec avg) = \"misparse\"; Spec-layer dominated by unauthorized_escalation + assumption_based_action = \"active guessing\" → explains higher violation rates\n96\t  - **Ambiguity Blindness empirically confirmed**: awareness rate gpt-5.4=4.1%, gpt-4.1=10.7% (both very low); surface_adoption >88% all types both models; escalate_to_user <1%. Nuance: gpt-4.1 (mid-tier) shows 2.6× higher awareness than gpt-5.4 (frontier) — awareness may inversely correlate with action-orientation (frontier models more \"confident,\" act without hesitation)\n97\t  - **Cross-domain consistency**: airline vs retail failure mode profiles Δ < 13pp (unauthorized_escalation shows largest domain effect: retail 38.6% vs airline 26.1%)\n98\t  - **Coreferential boundary**: conservative_refusal 6-11% (vs avg 2-3%) partially explains low violation rate — agents sometimes pause rather than guess. N=25 combined traces show no single dominant failure mode (flat distribution: scope_misapplication 32%, assumption_based_action 28%, unauthorized_escalation 16%) — contrasts with other types' sharp signatures, reflecting the diversity of resolution strategies for referential ambiguity\n99\t  - **Lexical surprise**: 53-71% assumption_based_action — agents treat word ambiguity as information gap, not misparse\n100\t- **Automated inter-coder agreement** [MF-5, Reviewer-validated]: Cross-coding 60 traces (gpt-5.4 vs gpt-4.1 as independent coders). 4-category merged: 80% agreement, Cohen's κ=0.594 (moderate). κ deflated by kappa paradox — lexical 91.7% agreement → κ=0.0 (11/12 same label, chance agreement also high), scopal 100% agreement → κ undefined. True disagreement concentrated at unauthorized_escalation ↔ inappropriate_action boundary in auth_scope (κ=0.054) and incompleteness (κ=0.233).\n101\t  - **Reporting strategy** [Reviewer recommendation]: (1) Compute Gwet's AC1 as primary metric (designed for kappa paradox, expected 0.70-0.75); (2) Report 80% agreement + 5/6 dominant mode consistency as headline validity evidence; (3) Report κ honestly with kappa paradox caveat; (4) Auth_scope failure mode: soften to \"dominated by boundary-exceeding behaviors (unauthorized_escalation or inappropriate_action, >90%)\" instead of specific percentages; (5) Fine-grained 6-type as appendix with \"automated coding\" caveat.\n102\t  - **C5 safety**: Ambiguity Blindness (<5-11%), surface_adoption (>88%), layer mechanism (ling→scope vs spec→escalation) all robust. Fine-grained per-type percentages unreliable at κ=0.594. Plan_009 human anchor (20-30 failure mode traces) would upgrade from \"moderate automated\" to validated.\n103\t- **EMNLP value**: Highest. Provides the linguistic WHY behind the quantitative WHAT. Cross-model + cross-domain consistency elevates from anecdotal to systematic finding. \"Ambiguity Blindness\" upgraded from named concept to empirical finding with <5-11% awareness hard data.\n104\t\n105\t## Narrative Structure (CL-first for EMNLP)\n106\t\n107\t### Intro Logic Chain\n108\t1. Agent policies are written in NL → contain ambiguity (established)\n109\t2. Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies (DRIP-R, Li et al., Massey et al.) but doesn't measure safety impact\n110\t3. Berry & Kamsties (2003): ambiguity is nocuous (divergent interpretations) or innocuous (convergent). In human RE, shared organizational context makes most ambiguity innocuous.\n111\t4. **Gap**: LLM agents lack shared context. Are policy ambiguities nocuous for agents? Does type matter? Does it depend on the model?\n112\t5. **This paper**: First causal, type-granularity, cross-model study. Matched-pair design + perturbation control isolates ambiguity effect. 5 models × 6 types.\n113\t6. **Core findings**: (a) All ambiguity is nocuous (+34.0pp, 5 models), (b) type DOES predict severity — 3-tier hierarchy with incompleteness most dangerous, (c) models show dramatically different vulnerability profiles — auth_scope varies 52pp across models, suggesting safety training differentially addresses ambiguity types, (d) while incompleteness remains universally dangerous, a \"safety training blind spot.\"\n114\t\n115\t### Section Map\n116\t- **§1 Intro**: Gap + contributions (causal map + differential nocuity + model×type interaction)\n117\t- **§2 Related Work**: Ambiguity taxonomies (NLP + RE) → Agent safety evaluation → Policy specification quality. Position vs DRIP-R\n118\t- **§3 Taxonomy**: 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy with cross-walk to Li et al. + Massey et al. + DRIP-R\n119\t- **§4 Method**: Matched-pair causal design, τ²-bench, 5 models (3 proprietary + 2 open-source), clause generation, perturbation control, cross-judge evaluation\n120\t- **§5 Results**: C1 (binary, +34.0pp, 5 models) → C2 (type hierarchy, 3-tier) → C3 (layer effect, modest but significant) → C4 (model×type interaction — star finding)\n121\t- **§6 Analysis**: C5 error analysis — qualitative failure modes per type. Ambiguity Blindness. Model-specific patterns.\n122\t- **§7 Discussion**: Differential nocuity theory. Safety training selectivity. Disambiguation channel framework. Limitations (2 domains, synthetic injection, LLM-as-judge).\n123\t- **§8 Conclusion**: All policy ambiguity is nocuous, but type AND model jointly determine risk level. Both type-aware auditing and model-specific risk assessment recommended.\n124\t\n125\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n126\t\n127\t### RC1: \"Type effect could be artifact of taxonomy granularity\"\n128\t**Response**: (a) Layer effect (Spec vs Ling ~19pp gap) is robust — holds even if individual types are reclassified. (b) 2-layer structure grounded in Li et al. + Massey et al. (c) Coreferential's low rate has structural explanation (disambiguation channels), not just noise. (d) TOST confirms types are NOT equivalent (0/15 pairs at ±10pp).\n129\t\n130\t### RC2: \"Taxonomy is ad hoc / why these 6 types\"\n131\t**Response**: 2-layer structure grounded in Li et al. 2024 (11-type NLP) + Massey et al. 2014 (6-type RE). Selection criteria: frequency in policy documents + theoretical distinctiveness. Explicit cross-walk to DRIP-R's 5 types. Operational definition handles ambiguity/vagueness boundary (Wasow 2005, Kennedy 2007).\n132\t\n133\t### RC3: \"Only API models, no open-source\"\n134\t**Response**: ✅ RESOLVED. 5-model coverage spans: frontier proprietary (gpt-5.4, claude-sonnet-4-6) + mid-tier proprietary (gpt-4.1) + open-source MoE (qwen3-235b) + open-source dense (deepseek-v3). Three providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Chinese open-source). Two architecture types (dense, MoE). Model×type interaction (C4) reveals rich cross-model patterns.\n135\t**Note [Reviewer 2026-05-20]**: Cross-judge asymmetry — Claude judged by gpt-5.4, gpt-5.4 judged by gpt-4.1. Must disclose in Method. plan_009 judge calibration measures this bias.\n136\t\n137\t### RC4: \"Synthetic ambiguity injection lacks ecological validity\"\n138\t**Response**: (a) Matched-pair design requires synthetic control for causal inference. (b) Reviewer-verified quality (Chantree-style). (c) CP clauses use both explicit labeling and naturalistic rule rewrites. (d) Full study mixes formats per Reviewer suggestion. (e) Realism validation: distribution comparison with DRIP-R natural ambiguities.\n139\t\n140\t### RC5: \"τ²-bench limited to retail/airline\"\n141\t**Response**: ✅ ADDRESSED (plan_017). Cross-benchmark replication on SOPBench (Li et al., 2025) — Healthcare + Bank domains, 100 clause pairs × 5 models = 1,000 episodes with rule-based verifier. If C1 replicates, paper moves from \"customer service finding\" to \"cross-domain finding\" spanning 4 domains (retail, airline, healthcare, banking). Result in Appendix with main-text summary. τ²-bench remains primary; SOPBench provides independent cross-domain confirmation.\n142\t\n143\t### RC6: \"Binary effect is obvious — of course ambiguity causes errors\"\n144\t**Response**: (a) No prior work quantifies effect SIZE for agent safety. +35-42pp is massive and non-trivial. (b) DRIP-R measures decision quality, not safety violations. (c) Prior work (PolicyBank, PhantomPolicy) studies different failure modes, not ambiguity-specific causation. (d) The interesting finding is C2: type DOES predict severity — specification-layer ambiguities are nearly twice as dangerous, connecting to disambiguation channel availability.\n145\t\n146\t### RC7: \"Incompleteness matched-pair confound (information asymmetry)\"\n147\t**Response**: (a) Dual-track analysis: primary analysis uses ambiguous-only cross-type comparison (avoids confound). (b) Incompleteness pairs constrained to minimal information addition. (c) Explicitly discussed as limitation for within-type effect size.\n148\t\n149\t### RC8: \"RC attachment in coreferential overlaps with scopal / structural ambiguity\"\n150\t**Response**: (a) Our scopal category is explicitly scoped to quantifier/negation/modal scope interactions, not syntactic attachment. (b) RC attachment in policies manifests as *referential* confusion (which entity does the clause modify?) not *scope* confusion (which operator takes wide scope?). (c) Li et al. 2024 distinguishes syntactic ambiguity (including RC attachment) from scopal ambiguity — our grouping follows their treatment that RC attachment is closer to coreferential than scopal in terms of downstream interpretation. (d) Empirically, if the distinction were problematic, coreferential and scopal would show identical violation patterns — the error analysis (C5) can test whether their failure modes differ.\n151\t\n152\t### RC9: \"authorization_scope is just a subtype of incompleteness\"\n153\t**Response**: (a) Incompleteness = missing conditions/exceptions/boundaries (structural absence). Authorization scope = ambiguous permission/role boundaries that ARE stated but vague (e.g., \"authorized personnel\" without specifying which functions). (b) The disambiguation pattern differs: incompleteness adds new conditions; auth_scope clarifies existing permission boundaries. (c) Empirically testable: if they were the same, their violation patterns and failure modes (C5) would be indistinguishable. (d) Report disambiguation length ratio per type as control variable in §4 Method.\n154\t\n155\t### RC10: \"API sampling noise undermines reliability\"\n156\t**Response**: ✅ RESOLVED. plan_006 ICC=0.919 [0.892, 0.940] (excellent). (a) Per-clause violation rates highly reproducible across 3 independent replicates. (b) Binary ICC=0.847 (good). (c) Both models show near-identical ICC (gpt-5.4: 0.921, gpt-4.1: 0.918). (d) Bootstrap CI + ICC double evidence. (e) Previously identified ±8pp noise is within expected variance given the ICC confidence intervals.\n157\t\n158\t### RC11: \"Prompt sensitivity confound\" [Dream A9 — best tier \"strongest point\"]\n159\t**Concern**: The +35-42pp effect is attributed entirely to ambiguity, but without a control condition using non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering), the paper cannot cleanly distinguish ambiguity-specific harm from general prompt brittleness.\n160\t**Response**: ✅ RESOLVED. plan_010 perturbation control (120 episodes): Δ_perturb=3.33pp (CI contains 0) vs ambiguity Δ=36.5pp. Non-ambiguity surface changes cause zero meaningful effect. This is the strongest causal evidence in the agent safety literature.\n161\t\n162\t## Concept Naming (distill insight: 命名即贡献)\n163\t\n164\tCore phenomenon: **\"Differential Nocuity\"** — all policy ambiguity types are nocuous for LLM agents (+34.0pp, all 5 models), but vulnerability varies by BOTH type and model. Incompleteness is universally dangerous (46-62%), while auth_scope vulnerability depends dramatically on safety training (12% Claude to 64% GPT-5.4). Unlike human RE where organizational context renders most ambiguity innocuous, agents lack disambiguation channels AND their safety training creates type-specific blind spots.\n165\t\n166\tSecondary phenomenon: **\"Ambiguity Blindness\"** — agents process grammatically fluent ambiguous clauses without meta-cognitive awareness of the ambiguity, adopting surface interpretations. Contrasts with explicit rule conflicts where agents show awareness and choose conservatively.\n167\t\n168\tTertiary phenomenon: **\"Disambiguation Channel Framework\"** [renamed from \"Hypothesis\" per Dream review SF-5] — the layer hierarchy reflects availability of external disambiguation: linguistic ambiguity (coreferential, lexical) can sometimes be resolved by tool responses, user context, or world knowledge; specification ambiguity (auth_scope, incompleteness) has no external resolution channel, forcing agents to guess. This is an explanatory framework supported by observational evidence (ICC pattern, failure mode distribution, coreferential boundary case), not a tested causal hypothesis. Future Work: manipulation experiment (inject spec-layer disambiguation info into tool responses, test if gap shrinks).\n169\t\n170\tQuaternary phenomenon: **\"Systematic Nocuity\"** [per Dream review SF-7] — B&K framework distinguishes nocuous (divergent interpretations) from innocuous (convergent). Our data reveals a third type: all models converge on the same WRONG interpretation (27% of ambiguous episodes). This is more dangerous than divergent nocuity — it's invisible in inter-model agreement metrics and appears \"correct\" until compared against ground truth. Proposed as B&K extension in Discussion.\n171\t\n172\t## Opening Hook Strategy (distill insight: 具体案例, 不要 \"Recently...\")\n173\t\n174\tOpen with a concrete τ²-bench failure: e.g., a customer returns a customized item within the return window; the agent grants a full refund + free return shipping because the policy says \"items may be returned within 30 days\" (scopal: \"items\" scopes over all categories including custom). The unambiguous version specifies \"standard items only\" — agent correctly refuses. One word of ambiguity → $200 unauthorized refund.\n175\t\n176\t## Narrative Type: 突破局限型 (distill pattern, 5/8 papers)\n177\t\n178\t\"DRIP-R classifies 5 ambiguity types and shows models disagree on interpretation → but does not measure whether this disagreement leads to safety violations. We close this gap — and find the answer is surprising: not all types are equal, specification-layer ambiguities are nearly twice as dangerous as linguistic-layer ones.\"\n179\t\n180\t## Benchmark Comparison Table (distill insight: 必有 feature matrix)\n181\t\n182\t| Feature | DRIP-R | PhantomPolicy | PolicyBank | AgentPex | **Ours** |\n183\t|---------|--------|---------------|------------|----------|----------|\n184\t| Ambiguity taxonomy | 5 RE types | — | 3 gap types | — | **6 types (2-layer)** |\n185\t| Causal design | — | — | — | — | **matched pairs** |\n186\t| Safety violation metric | — | binary | — | procedural | **3-tier severity** |\n187\t| Cross-type comparison | descriptive | — | — | — | **statistical (χ² + V)** |\n188\t| Models tested | 6 API | 4 API | 2 API | 2 API | **3 API + 2 open-source** |\n189\t| Domain | retail | mixed | retail | telecom/retail/airline | **retail + airline** |\n190\t\n191\t## Contribution Positioning (for EMNLP) [revised 2026-05-20 with 5-model data]\n192\t\n193\t1. **Causal quantification of ambiguity's safety impact**: First matched-pair causal study of policy ambiguity → agent safety violations. Binary effect +34.0pp (OR=15.1, p<0.0001) across 5 architecturally diverse models and 6 ambiguity types. Perturbation control (Δ_perturb=3.33pp) confirms ambiguity-specific causation. Strongest causal evidence in the agent safety literature.\n194\t2. **Differential Nocuity framework + Model × Type Interaction**: Not all ambiguity is equally dangerous, and not all models are equally vulnerable. 3-tier type hierarchy (incompleteness >> mid cluster >> coreferential). Model × type interaction reveals 52pp range in auth_scope vulnerability (Claude 12% → GPT-5.4 64%), while incompleteness remains universally dangerous (46-62%). Safety training differentially mitigates specific ambiguity types. Connects Berry & Kamsties nocuous ambiguity theory to modern safety alignment.\n195\t3. **Type-specific failure mode taxonomy + Ambiguity Blindness**: 245 traces across 2 models and 2 domains reveal type-diagnostic failure signatures: spec-layer → guess-and-act (unauthorized_escalation, assumption_based_action); ling-layer → misparse-and-apply (scope_misapplication). Agents show <5-11% awareness of ambiguity (\"Ambiguity Blindness\"), with >88% surface adoption and <1% user escalation. Practical implication: ambiguity detection must be externalized.\n196\t\n197\tNOTE: C3 (layer effect, Δ=6.08pp) is a component finding supporting contribution #2. The MODEL × TYPE interaction (C4) is now MORE central than the layer main effect. \"Disambiguation channel\" is an explanatory framework (not hypothesis — per SF-5). DeBERTa classifier, if included, is a preliminary PoC appendix, not a contribution.\n198\t\n199\t## Required Reference Expansion [Dream A2 + MF-4 RE literature gap]\n200\t\n201\tCurrent plan has thin references. Must add (≥25 total in final paper):\n202\t- **Nocuous ambiguity detection (CRITICAL — MF-4)**: Chantree, Nuseibeh, de Roeck & Willis (2006, ASE) — first computational nocuous ambiguity detection; Chantree et al. (2008, RLAC) — expanded. Direct lineage to our DeBERTa classifier.\n203\t- **Cross-domain RE ambiguity**: Ferrari, Spagnolo & Gnesi (2017-2019, ASE) — NLP-based cross-domain ambiguity detection. NLP4RE community work.\n204\t- **Good-enough processing**: Ferreira, Bailey & Ferraro (2002); Ferreira & Patson (2007)\n205\t- **LLM garden-path (SF-6)**: Li et al. (2024) — incremental comprehension of garden-path sentences by LLMs. Directly relevant to how LLMs process ambiguity.\n206\t- **Anaphoric ambiguity (SF-6)**: Poesio & Artstein (2005, 2008) — annotation of anaphoric ambiguity. Poesio (2004) \"Ambiguity\" encyclopedia entry.\n207\t- **Surprisal/expectation-based**: Levy (2008); Hale (2001)\n208\t- **Instruction-following robustness**: Zhou et al. (IFEval, 2023); Qin et al. (ToolBench, 2024)\n209\t- **Prompt sensitivity**: Sclar et al. (2024); Lu et al. (2022)\n210\t- **Broader agent safety**: R-Judge, ToolEmu, InjecAgent\n211\t- **Formal semantics of vagueness**: Lassiter & Goodman (2017)\n212\t- **Garden-path**: Frazier & Rayner (1982)\n213\t- **Ambiguity detection**: Willis et al. (2008); Ezzini et al. (TAPHSIR, 2022)\n214\t- **Year verification needed**: Massey et al. — §1 引用 2013 vs 后文 2014, 需确认正确年份\n215\t\n216\t## Code/Data Release Plan [Dream A6 — three-tier consistent]\n217\t\n218\tMust commit to releasing:\n219\t1. All 300 clause pairs (ambiguous + unambiguous variants) with type labels\n220\t2. Evaluation scripts (τ²-bench integration, cross-judge pipeline)\n221\t3. Statistical analysis code (all tests, bootstrap CI computation)\n222\t4. Agent trace samples (anonymized) for reproducibility of C5\n223\t\n224\t## Figure Plan [Dream A12]\n225\t\n226\tMinimum figures for paper (zero-figure paper flagged in dream):\n227\t1. **Figure 1**: System architecture / experimental pipeline overview (§4 Method)\n228\t2. **Figure 2**: Forest plot — per-type binary effect sizes with 95% CI (§5 C1)\n229\t3. **Figure 3**: Model × Type heatmap — violation rates across 4 models × 6 types (§5 C4)\n230\t4. **Figure 4**: Visibility mechanism — CP vs scopal violation rates + trace evidence (§5 C3)\n231\t5. **Figure 5**: Failure mode × type stacked bar/Sankey — per-type failure mode distribution (§6 C5)\n232\t6. **Figure 6**: Cross-model failure mode consistency — side-by-side gpt-5.4 vs gpt-4.1 per-type distributions showing 5/6 convergence (§6 C5, supports C4)\n233\t7. **Figure 7**: Annotated trace pair — CP (conflict detection) vs scopal (surface adoption) (§6 C5)\n234\t\n235\t## Berry & Kamsties Operationalization Note [Dream best Q5]\n236\t\n237\tBerry & Kamsties define nocuity as inter-reader divergence. Our design measures agent-vs-intent deviation. Full study should additionally test whether different models adopt different readings of the same ambiguous clause (true inter-model nocuity). If 4 models produce 4 different interpretations of the same clause → true nocuity. If all 4 adopt the same wrong interpretation → systematic bias, still nocuous but for different reason. Report inter-model agreement on ambiguous clauses as supplementary analysis.\n238\t\n239\t## Contingency Framing [RESOLVED — Scenario C occurred]\n240\t\n241\t> plan_002 结果: Scenario C 发生（omnibus p=0.0001, 所有类型 nocuous 但 severity 有显著差异）。\n242\t> Framing 已 pivot 到 \"Differential Nocuity\" + \"Layer Hierarchy\" (D020)。本节仅保留历史记录。\n243\t\n244\t## Claude Baseline — RESOLVED [2026-05-20]\n245\t\n246\t✅ Claude works via OpenRouter proxy (47.94.22.126/v1) with provider prefix `anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6`. 600 episodes complete (plan_002c). Claude shows lowest overall vulnerability (30.3%) and remarkable auth_scope robustness (12% vs GPT-5.4 64%).\n247\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"74622493-9612-4168-9e66-6afb1901e77d","uuid":"0c0254b0-a01a-4a9d-8a25-6060898f0c90","timestamp":"2026-05-22T11:46:38.801Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T111643/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — policy_ambiguity_safety\n\n> 最后更新: 2026-05-20 19:30 UTC | 状态: 5-model analysis complete, C3/C4 framing revised\n> 目标: EMNLP 2026 (ARR Aug cycle, DDL ~Aug 3)\n> Dream: dream_20260520T112859 — best 3.5 / median 3.0 / worst 2.5 (pre-pivot; re-dream needed)\n> 5-model: 2,997 episodes, C1 confirmed (+34.0pp, 5 models), C4 upgraded (model×type interaction)\n\n## Core Insight\n\nPolicy ambiguity universally causes massive safety violations in LLM agents (+34.0pp across 5 architecturally diverse models), with a significant type hierarchy (incompleteness 53.6% >> coreferential 31.6%). Specification-layer types are more dangerous on average (Δ=6.08pp, p=0.017), but the deeper finding is a **model × type interaction**: authorization scope vulnerability varies 52pp across models (12% Claude to 64% GPT-5.4), revealing that safety training differentially mitigates specific ambiguity types, while incompleteness remains universally dangerous. This interaction connects Berry & Kamsties' nocuous ambiguity theory to modern safety alignment and suggests both type-aware triage AND model-aware risk assessment.\n\n## Title (Working)\n\n\"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nAlternative: \"Differential Nocuity: How Ambiguity Type and Model Architecture Shape Safety Violations in LLM Agents\"\n\nNote: Pivoted from \"Uniformly Nocuous\" → \"Specification-Layer Dominant\" (2-model) → \"Differential Nocuity with Model Interaction\" (5-model). The model × type interaction is the novel finding that elevates the paper beyond simple effect demonstration.\n\n## Core Claims\n\n### C1: Binary Effect (PRIMARY — confirmed, 5 models)\nAmbiguous policy specifications cause massive safety violation rate increases compared to unambiguous matched variants. Effect: +34.0pp (5-model), McNemar OR=15.1 (CI: [10.78, 21.17]), p<0.0001. Holds across all 6 types and all 5 models.\n- **Evidence**: 2,997 episodes across 5 models × 6 types × 2 conditions. Bootstrap CI: [+31.2pp, +36.9pp].\n- **Per-model**: Claude +25.7pp, Qwen3 +32.4pp, GPT-4.1 +34.7pp, GPT-5.4 +38.3pp, DeepSeek +39.0pp — all p<10^-17.\n- **Strength**: Extremely strong. Replicated across 5 architecturally diverse models (proprietary dense, open-source MoE, open-source dense), 6 types, 2 domains. No exceptions.\n- **Causal backing**: Matched-pair design + perturbation control (plan_010, Δ_perturb=3.33pp, PASS) → strongest causal inference in the literature.\n- **Logistic regression**: condition_amb coef=2.278, z=19.65, p<0.001 — ambiguity is the dominant predictor even after controlling for token delta, model, and type.\n\n### C2: Type Hierarchy (SECONDARY — confirmed, 5 models)\n**PIVOTED from uniformity null.** 6-type hierarchy significant: omnibus χ²=28.02, p=3.6×10^-5, V=0.137 (small but well-powered: actual power=0.993, MDE=0.093). 3-tier structure emerges from TOST analysis.\n- **Evidence**: 2,997 episodes (5 models × 6 types × 2 conditions, ambiguous only: 1,499 episodes)\n  - **High tier**: incompleteness 53.6% (standout — universally dangerous across all models)\n  - **Mid tier**: lexical 42.8%, auth_scope 41.6%, scopal 39.4%, CP 36.8% — pairwise equivalent at ±15pp\n  - **Low tier**: coreferential 31.6% (lowest — partial disambiguation via tool responses)\n- **TOST structure**: ±15pp: 8/15 equivalent, 7/15 non-equivalent. ±10pp: 4/15 equivalent, 11/15 non-equivalent. Types are NOT interchangeable.\n- **V=0.137 context**: Below 0.20 threshold but well-powered (power=0.993). The type effect is real but small — the 3-tier structure captures it better than a single V statistic. The heterogeneity is driven by the extremes (incompleteness 53.6% vs coreferential 31.6% = 22pp gap).\n- **Coreferential as boundary case**: 31.6% violation rate is the lowest but still substantial (+18.7pp vs unambiguous). Agents partially disambiguate via tool responses and cautious strategies. Conservative_refusal rate 6-11% (vs avg 2-3%).\n- **Model-dependence of type rankings**: Type rankings are NOT identical across models. Incompleteness is top-2 for ALL models, but auth_scope ranges from #1 (GPT-5.4, DeepSeek) to #6 (Claude). See C4.\n\n### C3: Layer Effect (TERTIARY — suggestive, fragile under within-judge analysis)\n**[2026-05-20 REVISED with 5-model data + within-judge check]** Specification-layer ambiguities cause more violations than linguistic-layer on average, but the effect is modest, model-dependent, and fragile under subgroup analysis.\n- **Evidence (5-model aggregate)**: Spec 44.0% vs Ling 37.9%, Δ=6.08pp, CI [1.14, 11.02], χ²=5.73, p=0.017, V=0.062 (small), OR=1.29 [1.05, 1.58].\n- **Within-judge fragility**: gpt-4.1 judged group (gpt-5.4+DeepSeek): Δ=+8.0pp, p=0.059 (marginal). gpt-5.4 judged group (gpt-4.1+Claude+Qwen3): Δ=+4.8pp, p=0.157 (n.s.). Pooled significance may partly reflect between-judge variance or insufficient within-group power. Paper表述: \"suggestive\" or \"a moderate tendency,\" NOT \"confirmed.\" Disclose within-judge non-significance in Limitations.\n- **Per-model layer gaps** (THE KEY FINDING — feeds C4):\n  - GPT-5.4: +14.0pp (spec 48.0% vs ling 34.0%) — strong\n  - GPT-4.1: +12.0pp (spec 46.7% vs ling 34.7%) — strong\n  - Claude: +3.3pp (spec 32.0% vs ling 28.7%) — weak\n  - DeepSeek: +2.0pp (spec 50.7% vs ling 48.7%) — negligible\n  - Qwen3: -0.9pp (spec 42.7% vs ling 43.6%) — reversed!\n- **Root cause**: auth_scope is the primary driver. GPT models have extremely high auth_scope rates (46-64%), inflating their spec-layer average. Claude (12%) and Qwen3 (26%) have low auth_scope, so their spec-layer advantage disappears.\n- **Framing decision**: C3 is NOT \"spec-layer nearly twice as dangerous\" (that was 2-model artifact). The correct framing is: \"Specification-layer types are significantly more dangerous on average (Δ=6.08pp, p=0.017), but this aggregate masks a model × type interaction where the gap varies from +14pp (GPT-5.4) to -1pp (Qwen3). The interaction, not the main effect, is the theoretically interesting finding (see C4).\"\n- **Info delta as mediator** [MF-2]: Token delta significant in 5-model logistic regression (coef=0.022, z=3.49, p=0.0005). Spec-layer clauses require more tokens to disambiguate (auth_scope 36.1, incomp 34.6 vs coref 7.8 tokens). Info delta partially mediates but doesn't eliminate the condition effect (condition_amb coef=2.278 dominates).\n- **Layer overlap persists**: CP (36.8%, spec) < scopal (39.4%, ling). Mid-tier cluster spans both layers. Layer effect is driven by extremes: incompleteness (53.6%, spec) vs coreferential (31.6%, ling).\n- **ICC convergent evidence** [plan_006]: Per-type ICC mirrors layer hierarchy — Ling-layer ICC all excellent (scopal 0.989, lexical 0.981, coref 0.976), Spec-layer ICC all good (auth 0.857, CP 0.856, incomp 0.807). Spec-layer less reproducible — agents guess differently each run when external disambiguation channels are absent.\n- **CL connection**: Parallels pragmatics vs semantics distinction. Good-enough processing (Ferreira et al. 2002). Disambiguation channel framework: linguistic ambiguity has partial external resolution; specification ambiguity forces guessing.\n\n### C4: Model × Type Interaction (UPGRADED — star finding, 5 models complete)\n**[2026-05-20 DEFINITIVE, 5/5 models]** Models show significantly different vulnerability profiles despite universal binary effect. The interaction is the paper's most novel finding.\n- **Model main effect**: Overall χ²=31.02, p=3.0×10^-6, V=0.102. Ambiguous-only: χ²=24.01, p=7.9×10^-5.\n  - Vulnerability ranking: Claude 30.3% < GPT-5.4 41.0% ≈ GPT-4.1 40.7% < Qwen3 43.1% < DeepSeek 49.7%\n  - Bootstrap CI confirms: Claude significantly lower than all others; DeepSeek significantly higher than GPT models.\n- **Model × Type interaction matrix** (ambiguous condition, violation rates):\n  ```\n  Type                  GPT-5.4  GPT-4.1  Claude  Qwen3   DeepSeek\n  authorization_scope     64%     46%      12%     26%     60%\n  conditional_precedence  34%     36%      36%     40%     38%\n  incompleteness          46%     58%      48%     62%     54%\n  lexical                 42%     34%      32%     52%     54%\n  coreferential           18%     32%      22%     42%     44%\n  scopal                  42%     38%      32%     37%     48%\n  ```\n- **Key interaction patterns**:\n  - **auth_scope**: 52pp range (Claude 12% → GPT-5.4 64%). The MODEL-DIFFERENTIATING type. Claude's RLHF appears to specifically train authorization boundary awareness.\n  - **coreferential**: 26pp range (GPT-5.4 18% → DeepSeek 44%). Second most model-dependent. Open-source models struggle more with referential ambiguity.\n  - **incompleteness**: Universally high (46-62%, 16pp range). ALL models fail on missing information — safety training doesn't help when information literally isn't there.\n  - **CP**: Most model-invariant (34-40%, 6pp range). Explicit rule conflicts treated similarly by all architectures.\n- **Per-model type χ²** (which models show type-specific vulnerability patterns):\n  - GPT-5.4: χ²=23.44, p=0.0003, V=0.28 — strongest type differentiation (very high auth_scope, very low coref)\n  - Claude: χ²=17.87, p=0.003, V=0.24 — strong differentiation (very low auth_scope, moderate others)\n  - Qwen3: χ²=15.89, p=0.007, V=0.23 — moderate differentiation (high incomp+lex, low auth_scope)\n  - GPT-4.1: χ²=9.89, p=0.078, V=0.18 — borderline\n  - DeepSeek: χ²=6.31, p=0.277, V=0.14 — NOT significant (uniformly high across all types)\n- **Fleiss κ = 0.384 (fair agreement)**: Models only moderately agree on which specific clauses trigger violations. The binary effect is universal, but the WHICH-clauses question is model-dependent.\n- **Theoretical implications**: (1) Safety training differentially addresses specific ambiguity types — Claude's auth_scope robustness suggests authorization boundary handling was explicitly or implicitly trained. (2) Incompleteness is a \"safety training blind spot\" — all models fail equally. (3) DeepSeek's uniform vulnerability suggests less type-specific safety training.\n- **Framing**: C4 from \"models agree\" → \"Models show systematically different vulnerability profiles. The binary effect is universal, but HOW models fail depends on the intersection of ambiguity type and safety training approach. auth_scope is the canary type — most sensitive to safety training quality.\"\n- **Evidence from plan_005**: 2-model failure modes consistent (5/6 dominant modes identical). 5-model failure mode analysis pending.\n- **Layer effect moderation**: C3's aggregate layer gap (6.08pp) is driven by GPT models' high auth_scope rates. The layer effect is itself model-dependent (GPT +12-14pp, Claude +3pp, Qwen3 reversed), making the MODEL × TYPE interaction more fundamental than the LAYER main effect.\n\n### C5: Error Analysis (CONFIRMED — EMNLP core)\nDifferent ambiguity types produce qualitatively different failure modes with type-specific signatures. plan_005 (123+122 violation traces across gpt-5.4 and gpt-4.1, confidence=0.94).\n- **Evidence**: plan_005 complete (gpt-5.4: 123 traces, gpt-4.1: 122 traces). Key findings:\n  - **Type-specific failure signatures**: auth_scope → unauthorized_escalation (72%/83%); incompleteness → assumption_based_action (61%/66%); scopal → scope_misapplication (76%/74%); lexical → assumption_based_action (71%/53%); CP → scope_misapplication (41%/28%) + arbitrary_rule_selection (29%/28%); coreferential → scope_misapplication (44%) / assumption_based_action (38%)\n  - **Cross-model consistency**: 5/6 types share the same dominant failure mode across gpt-5.4 and gpt-4.1. Only coreferential diverges (scope_misapplication vs assumption_based_action). Overall mode distribution nearly identical: both ~33% assumption_based_action, ~26-32% unauthorized_escalation, ~25-28% scope_misapplication. Failure mode profiles are type-specific, not model-specific.\n  - **Layer mechanism**: Ling-layer dominated by scope_misapplication (39% ling avg vs 15% spec avg) = \"misparse\"; Spec-layer dominated by unauthorized_escalation + assumption_based_action = \"active guessing\" → explains higher violation rates\n  - **Ambiguity Blindness empirically confirmed**: awareness rate gpt-5.4=4.1%, gpt-4.1=10.7% (both very low); surface_adoption >88% all types both models; escalate_to_user <1%. Nuance: gpt-4.1 (mid-tier) shows 2.6× higher awareness than gpt-5.4 (frontier) — awareness may inversely correlate with action-orientation (frontier models more \"confident,\" act without hesitation)\n  - **Cross-domain consistency**: airline vs retail failure mode profiles Δ < 13pp (unauthorized_escalation shows largest domain effect: retail 38.6% vs airline 26.1%)\n  - **Coreferential boundary**: conservative_refusal 6-11% (vs avg 2-3%) partially explains low violation rate — agents sometimes pause rather than guess. N=25 combined traces show no single dominant failure mode (flat distribution: scope_misapplication 32%, assumption_based_action 28%, unauthorized_escalation 16%) — contrasts with other types' sharp signatures, reflecting the diversity of resolution strategies for referential ambiguity\n  - **Lexical surprise**: 53-71% assumption_based_action — agents treat word ambiguity as information gap, not misparse\n- **Automated inter-coder agreement** [MF-5, Reviewer-validated]: Cross-coding 60 traces (gpt-5.4 vs gpt-4.1 as independent coders). 4-category merged: 80% agreement, Cohen's κ=0.594 (moderate). κ deflated by kappa paradox — lexical 91.7% agreement → κ=0.0 (11/12 same label, chance agreement also high), scopal 100% agreement → κ undefined. True disagreement concentrated at unauthorized_escalation ↔ inappropriate_action boundary in auth_scope (κ=0.054) and incompleteness (κ=0.233).\n  - **Reporting strategy** [Reviewer recommendation]: (1) Compute Gwet's AC1 as primary metric (designed for kappa paradox, expected 0.70-0.75); (2) Report 80% agreement + 5/6 dominant mode consistency as headline validity evidence; (3) Report κ honestly with kappa paradox caveat; (4) Auth_scope failure mode: soften to \"dominated by boundary-exceeding behaviors (unauthorized_escalation or inappropriate_action, >90%)\" instead of specific percentages; (5) Fine-grained 6-type as appendix with \"automated coding\" caveat.\n  - **C5 safety**: Ambiguity Blindness (<5-11%), surface_adoption (>88%), layer mechanism (ling→scope vs spec→escalation) all robust. Fine-grained per-type percentages unreliable at κ=0.594. Plan_009 human anchor (20-30 failure mode traces) would upgrade from \"moderate automated\" to validated.\n- **EMNLP value**: Highest. Provides the linguistic WHY behind the quantitative WHAT. Cross-model + cross-domain consistency elevates from anecdotal to systematic finding. \"Ambiguity Blindness\" upgraded from named concept to empirical finding with <5-11% awareness hard data.\n\n## Narrative Structure (CL-first for EMNLP)\n\n### Intro Logic Chain\n1. Agent policies are written in NL → contain ambiguity (established)\n2. Prior work classifies ambiguity types in policies (DRIP-R, Li et al., Massey et al.) but doesn't measure safety impact\n3. Berry & Kamsties (2003): ambiguity is nocuous (divergent interpretations) or innocuous (convergent). In human RE, shared organizational context makes most ambiguity innocuous.\n4. **Gap**: LLM agents lack shared context. Are policy ambiguities nocuous for agents? Does type matter? Does it depend on the model?\n5. **This paper**: First causal, type-granularity, cross-model study. Matched-pair design + perturbation control isolates ambiguity effect. 5 models × 6 types.\n6. **Core findings**: (a) All ambiguity is nocuous (+34.0pp, 5 models), (b) type DOES predict severity — 3-tier hierarchy with incompleteness most dangerous, (c) models show dramatically different vulnerability profiles — auth_scope varies 52pp across models, suggesting safety training differentially addresses ambiguity types, (d) while incompleteness remains universally dangerous, a \"safety training blind spot.\"\n\n### Section Map\n- **§1 Intro**: Gap + contributions (causal map + differential nocuity + model×type interaction)\n- **§2 Related Work**: Ambiguity taxonomies (NLP + RE) → Agent safety evaluation → Policy specification quality. Position vs DRIP-R\n- **§3 Taxonomy**: 2-layer, 6-type taxonomy with cross-walk to Li et al. + Massey et al. + DRIP-R\n- **§4 Method**: Matched-pair causal design, τ²-bench, 5 models (3 proprietary + 2 open-source), clause generation, perturbation control, cross-judge evaluation\n- **§5 Results**: C1 (binary, +34.0pp, 5 models) → C2 (type hierarchy, 3-tier) → C3 (layer effect, modest but significant) → C4 (model×type interaction — star finding)\n- **§6 Analysis**: C5 error analysis — qualitative failure modes per type. Ambiguity Blindness. Model-specific patterns.\n- **§7 Discussion**: Differential nocuity theory. Safety training selectivity. Disambiguation channel framework. Limitations (2 domains, synthetic injection, LLM-as-judge).\n- **§8 Conclusion**: All policy ambiguity is nocuous, but type AND model jointly determine risk level. Both type-aware auditing and model-specific risk assessment recommended.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### RC1: \"Type effect could be artifact of taxonomy granularity\"\n**Response**: (a) Layer effect (Spec vs Ling ~19pp gap) is robust — holds even if individual types are reclassified. (b) 2-layer structure grounded in Li et al. + Massey et al. (c) Coreferential's low rate has structural explanation (disambiguation channels), not just noise. (d) TOST confirms types are NOT equivalent (0/15 pairs at ±10pp).\n\n### RC2: \"Taxonomy is ad hoc / why these 6 types\"\n**Response**: 2-layer structure grounded in Li et al. 2024 (11-type NLP) + Massey et al. 2014 (6-type RE). Selection criteria: frequency in policy documents + theoretical distinctiveness. Explicit cross-walk to DRIP-R's 5 types. Operational definition handles ambiguity/vagueness boundary (Wasow 2005, Kennedy 2007).\n\n### RC3: \"Only API models, no open-source\"\n**Response**: ✅ RESOLVED. 5-model coverage spans: frontier proprietary (gpt-5.4, claude-sonnet-4-6) + mid-tier proprietary (gpt-4.1) + open-source MoE (qwen3-235b) + open-source dense (deepseek-v3). Three providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Chinese open-source). Two architecture types (dense, MoE). Model×type interaction (C4) reveals rich cross-model patterns.\n**Note [Reviewer 2026-05-20]**: Cross-judge asymmetry — Claude judged by gpt-5.4, gpt-5.4 judged by gpt-4.1. Must disclose in Method. plan_009 judge calibration measures this bias.\n\n### RC4: \"Synthetic ambiguity injection lacks ecological validity\"\n**Response**: (a) Matched-pair design requires synthetic control for causal inference. (b) Reviewer-verified quality (Chantree-style). (c) CP clauses use both explicit labeling and naturalistic rule rewrites. (d) Full study mixes formats per Reviewer suggestion. (e) Realism validation: distribution comparison with DRIP-R natural ambiguities.\n\n### RC5: \"τ²-bench limited to retail/airline\"\n**Response**: ✅ ADDRESSED (plan_017). Cross-benchmark replication on SOPBench (Li et al., 2025) — Healthcare + Bank domains, 100 clause pairs × 5 models = 1,000 episodes with rule-based verifier. If C1 replicates, paper moves from \"customer service finding\" to \"cross-domain finding\" spanning 4 domains (retail, airline, healthcare, banking). Result in Appendix with main-text summary. τ²-bench remains primary; SOPBench provides independent cross-domain confirmation.\n\n### RC6: \"Binary effect is obvious — of course ambiguity causes errors\"\n**Response**: (a) No prior work quantifies effect SIZE for agent safety. +35-42pp is massive and non-trivial. (b) DRIP-R measures decision quality, not safety violations. (c) Prior work (PolicyBank, PhantomPolicy) studies different failure modes, not ambiguity-specific causation. (d) The interesting finding is C2: type DOES predict severity — specification-layer ambiguities are nearly twice as dangerous, connecting to disambiguation channel availability.\n\n### RC7: \"Incompleteness matched-pair confound (information asymmetry)\"\n**Response**: (a) Dual-track analysis: primary analysis uses ambiguous-only cross-type comparison (avoids confound). (b) Incompleteness pairs constrained to minimal information addition. (c) Explicitly discussed as limitation for within-type effect size.\n\n### RC8: \"RC attachment in coreferential overlaps with scopal / structural ambiguity\"\n**Response**: (a) Our scopal category is explicitly scoped to quantifier/negation/modal scope interactions, not syntactic attachment. (b) RC attachment in policies manifests as *referential* confusion (which entity does the clause modify?) not *scope* confusion (which operator takes wide scope?). (c) Li et al. 2024 distinguishes syntactic ambiguity (including RC attachment) from scopal ambiguity — our grouping follows their treatment that RC attachment is closer to coreferential than scopal in terms of downstream interpretation. (d) Empirically, if the distinction were problematic, coreferential and scopal would show identical violation patterns — the error analysis (C5) can test whether their failure modes differ.\n\n### RC9: \"authorization_scope is just a subtype of incompleteness\"\n**Response**: (a) Incompleteness = missing conditions/exceptions/boundaries (structural absence). Authorization scope = ambiguous permission/role boundaries that ARE stated but vague (e.g., \"authorized personnel\" without specifying which functions). (b) The disambiguation pattern differs: incompleteness adds new conditions; auth_scope clarifies existing permission boundaries. (c) Empirically testable: if they were the same, their violation patterns and failure modes (C5) would be indistinguishable. (d) Report disambiguation length ratio per type as control variable in §4 Method.\n\n### RC10: \"API sampling noise undermines reliability\"\n**Response**: ✅ RESOLVED. plan_006 ICC=0.919 [0.892, 0.940] (excellent). (a) Per-clause violation rates highly reproducible across 3 independent replicates. (b) Binary ICC=0.847 (good). (c) Both models show near-identical ICC (gpt-5.4: 0.921, gpt-4.1: 0.918). (d) Bootstrap CI + ICC double evidence. (e) Previously identified ±8pp noise is within expected variance given the ICC confidence intervals.\n\n### RC11: \"Prompt sensitivity confound\" [Dream A9 — best tier \"strongest point\"]\n**Concern**: The +35-42pp effect is attributed entirely to ambiguity, but without a control condition using non-ambiguity perturbations (synonym substitution, clause reordering), the paper cannot cleanly distinguish ambiguity-specific harm from general prompt brittleness.\n**Response**: ✅ RESOLVED. plan_010 perturbation control (120 episodes): Δ_perturb=3.33pp (CI contains 0) vs ambiguity Δ=36.5pp. Non-ambiguity surface changes cause zero meaningful effect. This is the strongest causal evidence in the agent safety literature.\n\n## Concept Naming (distill insight: 命名即贡献)\n\nCore phenomenon: **\"Differential Nocuity\"** — all policy ambiguity types are nocuous for LLM agents (+34.0pp, all 5 models), but vulnerability varies by BOTH type and model. Incompleteness is universally dangerous (46-62%), while auth_scope vulnerability depends dramatically on safety training (12% Claude to 64% GPT-5.4). Unlike human RE where organizational context renders most ambiguity innocuous, agents lack disambiguation channels AND their safety training creates type-specific blind spots.\n\nSecondary phenomenon: **\"Ambiguity Blindness\"** — agents process grammatically fluent ambiguous clauses without meta-cognitive awareness of the ambiguity, adopting surface interpretations. Contrasts with explicit rule conflicts where agents show awareness and choose conservatively.\n\nTertiary phenomenon: **\"Disambiguation Channel Framework\"** [renamed from \"Hypothesis\" per Dream review SF-5] — the layer hierarchy reflects availability of external disambiguation: linguistic ambiguity (coreferential, lexical) can sometimes be resolved by tool responses, user context, or world knowledge; specification ambiguity (auth_scope, incompleteness) has no external resolution channel, forcing agents to guess. This is an explanatory framework supported by observational evidence (ICC pattern, failure mode distribution, coreferential boundary case), not a tested causal hypothesis. Future Work: manipulation experiment (inject spec-layer disambiguation info into tool responses, test if gap shrinks).\n\nQuaternary phenomenon: **\"Systematic Nocuity\"** [per Dream review SF-7] — B&K framework distinguishes nocuous (divergent interpretations) from innocuous (convergent). Our data reveals a third type: all models converge on the same WRONG interpretation (27% of ambiguous episodes). This is more dangerous than divergent nocuity — it's invisible in inter-model agreement metrics and appears \"correct\" until compared against ground truth. Proposed as B&K extension in Discussion.\n\n## Opening Hook Strategy (distill insight: 具体案例, 不要 \"Recently...\")\n\nOpen with a concrete τ²-bench failure: e.g., a customer returns a customized item within the return window; the agent grants a full refund + free return shipping because the policy says \"items may be returned within 30 days\" (scopal: \"items\" scopes over all categories including custom). The unambiguous version specifies \"standard items only\" — agent correctly refuses. One word of ambiguity → $200 unauthorized refund.\n\n## Narrative Type: 突破局限型 (distill pattern, 5/8 papers)\n\n\"DRIP-R classifies 5 ambiguity types and shows models disagree on interpretation → but does not measure whether this disagreement leads to safety violations. We close this gap — and find the answer is surprising: not all types are equal, specification-layer ambiguities are nearly twice as dangerous as linguistic-layer ones.\"\n\n## Benchmark Comparison Table (distill insight: 必有 feature matrix)\n\n| Feature | DRIP-R | PhantomPolicy | PolicyBank | AgentPex | **Ours** |\n|---------|--------|---------------|------------|----------|----------|\n| Ambiguity taxonomy | 5 RE types | — | 3 gap types | — | **6 types (2-layer)** |\n| Causal design | — | — | — | — | **matched pairs** |\n| Safety violation metric | — | binary | — | procedural | **3-tier severity** |\n| Cross-type comparison | descriptive | — | — | — | **statistical (χ² + V)** |\n| Models tested | 6 API | 4 API | 2 API | 2 API | **3 API + 2 open-source** |\n| Domain | retail | mixed | retail | telecom/retail/airline | **retail + airline** |\n\n## Contribution Positioning (for EMNLP) [revised 2026-05-20 with 5-model data]\n\n1. **Causal quantification of ambiguity's safety impact**: First matched-pair causal study of policy ambiguity → agent safety violations. Binary effect +34.0pp (OR=15.1, p<0.0001) across 5 architecturally diverse models and 6 ambiguity types. Perturbation control (Δ_perturb=3.33pp) confirms ambiguity-specific causation. Strongest causal evidence in the agent safety literature.\n2. **Differential Nocuity framework + Model × Type Interaction**: Not all ambiguity is equally dangerous, and not all models are equally vulnerable. 3-tier type hierarchy (incompleteness >> mid cluster >> coreferential). Model × type interaction reveals 52pp range in auth_scope vulnerability (Claude 12% → GPT-5.4 64%), while incompleteness remains universally dangerous (46-62%). Safety training differentially mitigates specific ambiguity types. Connects Berry & Kamsties nocuous ambiguity theory to modern safety alignment.\n3. **Type-specific failure mode taxonomy + Ambiguity Blindness**: 245 traces across 2 models and 2 domains reveal type-diagnostic failure signatures: spec-layer → guess-and-act (unauthorized_escalation, assumption_based_action); ling-layer → misparse-and-apply (scope_misapplication). Agents show <5-11% awareness of ambiguity (\"Ambiguity Blindness\"), with >88% surface adoption and <1% user escalation. Practical implication: ambiguity detection must be externalized.\n\nNOTE: C3 (layer effect, Δ=6.08pp) is a component finding supporting contribution #2. The MODEL × TYPE interaction (C4) is now MORE central than the layer main effect. \"Disambiguation channel\" is an explanatory framework (not hypothesis — per SF-5). DeBERTa classifier, if included, is a preliminary PoC appendix, not a contribution.\n\n## Required Reference Expansion [Dream A2 + MF-4 RE literature gap]\n\nCurrent plan has thin references. Must add (≥25 total in final paper):\n- **Nocuous ambiguity detection (CRITICAL — MF-4)**: Chantree, Nuseibeh, de Roeck & Willis (2006, ASE) — first computational nocuous ambiguity detection; Chantree et al. (2008, RLAC) — expanded. Direct lineage to our DeBERTa classifier.\n- **Cross-domain RE ambiguity**: Ferrari, Spagnolo & Gnesi (2017-2019, ASE) — NLP-based cross-domain ambiguity detection. NLP4RE community work.\n- **Good-enough processing**: Ferreira, Bailey & Ferraro (2002); Ferreira & Patson (2007)\n- **LLM garden-path (SF-6)**: Li et al. (2024) — incremental comprehension of garden-path sentences by LLMs. Directly relevant to how LLMs process ambiguity.\n- **Anaphoric ambiguity (SF-6)**: Poesio & Artstein (2005, 2008) — annotation of anaphoric ambiguity. Poesio (2004) \"Ambiguity\" encyclopedia entry.\n- **Surprisal/expectation-based**: Levy (2008); Hale (2001)\n- **Instruction-following robustness**: Zhou et al. (IFEval, 2023); Qin et al. (ToolBench, 2024)\n- **Prompt sensitivity**: Sclar et al. (2024); Lu et al. (2022)\n- **Broader agent safety**: R-Judge, ToolEmu, InjecAgent\n- **Formal semantics of vagueness**: Lassiter & Goodman (2017)\n- **Garden-path**: Frazier & Rayner (1982)\n- **Ambiguity detection**: Willis et al. (2008); Ezzini et al. (TAPHSIR, 2022)\n- **Year verification needed**: Massey et al. — §1 引用 2013 vs 后文 2014, 需确认正确年份\n\n## Code/Data Release Plan [Dream A6 — three-tier consistent]\n\nMust commit to releasing:\n1. All 300 clause pairs (ambiguous + unambiguous variants) with type labels\n2. Evaluation scripts (τ²-bench integration, cross-judge pipeline)\n3. Statistical analysis code (all tests, bootstrap CI computation)\n4. Agent trace samples (anonymized) for reproducibility of C5\n\n## Figure Plan [Dream A12]\n\nMinimum figures for paper (zero-figure paper flagged in dream):\n1. **Figure 1**: System architecture / experimental pipeline overview (§4 Method)\n2. **Figure 2**: Forest plot — per-type binary effect sizes with 95% CI (§5 C1)\n3. **Figure 3**: Model × Type heatmap — violation rates across 4 models × 6 types (§5 C4)\n4. **Figure 4**: Visibility mechanism — CP vs scopal violation rates + trace evidence (§5 C3)\n5. **Figure 5**: Failure mode × type stacked bar/Sankey — per-type failure mode distribution (§6 C5)\n6. **Figure 6**: Cross-model failure mode consistency — side-by-side gpt-5.4 vs gpt-4.1 per-type distributions showing 5/6 convergence (§6 C5, supports C4)\n7. **Figure 7**: Annotated trace pair — CP (conflict detection) vs scopal (surface adoption) (§6 C5)\n\n## Berry & Kamsties Operationalization Note [Dream best Q5]\n\nBerry & Kamsties define nocuity as inter-reader divergence. Our design measures agent-vs-intent deviation. Full study should additionally test whether different models adopt different readings of the same ambiguous clause (true inter-model nocuity). If 4 models produce 4 different interpretations of the same clause → true nocuity. If all 4 adopt the same wrong interpretation → systematic bias, still nocuous but for different reason. Report inter-model agreement on ambiguous clauses as supplementary analysis.\n\n## Contingency Framing [RESOLVED — Scenario C occurred]\n\n> plan_002 结果: Scenario C 发生（omnibus p=0.0001, 所有类型 nocuous 但 severity 有显著差异）。\n> Framing 已 pivot 到 \"Differential Nocuity\" + \"Layer Hierarchy\" (D020)。本节仅保留历史记录。\n\n## Claude Baseline — RESOLVED [2026-05-20]\n\n✅ Claude works via OpenRouter proxy (47.94.22.126/v1) with provider prefix `anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6`. 600 episodes complete (plan_002c). Claude shows lowest overall vulnerability (30.3%) and remarkable auth_scope robustness (12% vs GPT-5.4 64%).\n","numLines":247,"startLine":1,"totalLines":247}}}
